<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maurice, Prince of Orange</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1567–<a href="/years/1625">1625</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Maurice of Nassau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maurice of Nassau, Prince of Orange from 1618, was stadtholder of Holland and Zeeland from 1585 and later of most other provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>. A son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-silent">William the Silent</a>, he reformed the army with his cousin William Louis, introducing regular drill, smaller units and volley fire, and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-stevin">Simon Stevin</a> he recaptured many towns from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in the 1590s. He defeated a Spanish army at Nieuwpoort in 1600 and in 1619 had Oldenbarnevelt executed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maurice-prince-of-orange.jpg" alt="Maurice, Prince of Orange" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: School of Michiel Jansz. van Mierevelt · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:School_of_Michiel_Jansz._van_Mierevelt_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
